      \field{abstract}{Reasoning ability, a core component of human intelligence, continues to pose a significant challenge for Large Language Models (LLMs) in the pursuit of AGI. Although model performance has improved under the training scaling law, significant challenges remain, particularly with respect to training algorithms, such as catastrophic forgetting, and the limited availability of novel training data. As an alternative, test-time scaling enhances reasoning performance by increasing test-time computation without parameter updating. Unlike prior methods in this paradigm focused on token space, we propose leveraging latent space for more effective reasoning and better adherence to the test-time scaling law. We introduce LatentSeek, a novel framework that enhances LLM reasoning through Test-Time Instance-level Adaptation (TTIA) within the model's latent space. Specifically, LatentSeek leverages policy gradient to iteratively update latent representations, guided by self-generated reward signals. LatentSeek is evaluated on a range of reasoning benchmarks, including GSM8K, MATH-500, and AIME2024, across multiple LLM architectures. Results show that LatentSeek consistently outperforms strong baselines, such as Chain-of-Thought prompting and fine-tuning-based methods. Furthermore, our analysis demonstrates that LatentSeek is highly efficient, typically converging within a few iterations for problems of average complexity, while also benefiting from additional iterations, thereby highlighting the potential of test-time scaling in the latent space. These findings position LatentSeek as a lightweight, scalable, and effective solution for enhancing the reasoning capabilities of LLMs.}
      \field{annotation}{Seek in the Dark: Reasoning via Test-Time Instance-Level Policy Gradient in Latent Space Introduction Describes LATENTSEEK, a framework that iteratively updates latent representations of Large Language Models (LLMs) at test time to enhance reasoning capabilities. Explains Test-Time Instance-level Adaptation (TTIA), the framework within which LATENTSEEK operates. Mentions the advantages of TTIA and describes how LATENTSEEK leverages policy gradients to update latent representations guided by self-generated rewards. Test-Time Instance-Level Adaptation (TTIA) in Latent Space Presents the problem formulation for TTIA in reasoning tasks. Discusses the optimization of latent representations using policy gradients, and justifies the independence of latent representations with theoretical analysis. Describes the LATENTSEEK algorithm, including its decoding strategy, reward function, and techniques for stable and efficient optimization, such as CoT initialization and fractional sequence optimization. Empirical Results Presents the experimental setup, including the reward mechanism, prompt designation, backbone LLMs, benchmarks, and baselines used for evaluation. Presents the results on GSM8K, MATH-500, and AIME2024, highlighting the strong performance of LATENTSEEK compared to various baselines, including CoT prompting, Best-of-N, reinforcement learning methods, and supervised fine-tuning. Discusses the generalizability of LATENTSEEK across different LLM families and model sizes. Ideal Experiment: Perfect Sparse Reward Model Discusses experiments conducted using a Perfect Sparse Reward Model (PSRM) to evaluate the effectiveness of exploration within the latent space. Notes that the PSRM model leads to significant performance gains despite limited feedback, demonstrating the potential of LATENTSEEK even with sparse rewards. Mentions observations that suggest smaller models may lack effective knowledge elicitation mechanisms, and that LATENTSEEK can help activate implicit knowledge. Test-Time Scaling: scaling up the iteration of LATENTSEEK Proposes a novel test-time scaling approach by increasing the number of LATENTSEEK iterations rather than the number of generated tokens. Presents the relationship between model performance on GSM8K and the number of iterations, showing that performance improves with additional iterations. Discusses extreme scaling experiments using Qwen2. 5-1.5 B-Instruct, highlighting the potential of latent space for efficient test-time scaling. Algorithmic Statistics Presents statistics on the ratio of final answer tokens to CoT tokens and average number of update iterations. Highlights the efficiency of LATENTSEEK, showing that it typically converges within a few iterations for problems of average complexity. Qualitative Analysis Presents an analysis of the first three generated tokens in GSM8K, highlighting the divergence of LLM reasoning from human cognitive strategies. Provides case studies illustrating how LATENTSEEK produces correct answers despite generating seemingly incoherent reasoning steps. Related Work Briefly discusses related work on reasoning in language models, reinforcement learning for language models, controllable generation, test-time optimization, prompt tuning, and soft prompt. Conclusion Summarizes the contributions of LATENTSEEK as a novel, efficient, and scalable approach for enhancing reasoning in LLMs. Highlights the potential of TTIA in the latent space for improving LLM capabilities, and mentions future directions for research.}
