      \field{annotation}{Hierarchical Optimal Control of Nonlinear Systems; An Application to a Benchmark CSTR Problem Abstract Describes a novel computational algorithm for hierarchical optimal control of nonlinear systems. Presents a new coordination strategy based on the gradient of the coordination errors. Demonstrates the performance and convergence rate of the proposed approach through simulations of a benchmark continuous stirred tank reactor (CSTR) problem. Compares the proposed approach to the classical gradient-type interaction prediction approach. Introduction Notes the complexity and dimensionality problems of centralized optimal control in large-scale systems. Highlights the feasibility of hierarchical structures in reducing complexity through decomposition and parallel processing. Mentions two main hierarchical approaches for dynamic optimization problems: Goal Coordination Method and Interaction Prediction Approach. Discusses the limitations of the Goal Coordination Method and the applicability of the Interaction Prediction Approach. Statement of the Problem Presents a discrete-time dynamic system with bounded control inputs. Defines the optimal control problem as finding the control policy that minimizes a given performance index over a specified time interval. Decomposition of the Overall Problem into Sub-Problems Divides the system dynamics into interactive sub-systems with nonlinear state equations. Decomposes the overall cost function into separate cost functions for each sub-system. Defines the interaction vector for each sub-system as a function of the states and inputs of other sub-systems. Introduces the overall Lagrangian function and the necessary conditions for optimality. Optimization of the First Level Sub-Problems Restates the necessary conditions for optimality for the first level sub-problems. Outlines the steps of the gradient algorithm used to solve the sub-problems. Explains the process of updating the control inputs using the gradient of the Lagrangian function. Solving the Second Level Problem (Coordination) Defines the calculated coordination vector and the predicted coordination vector. Explains the classical gradient-type algorithm (mixed method) for modifying the coordination vector. Introduces a new gradient-type coordination approach based on the coordination error. Defines a new cost function based on the norm of the coordination error. Presents the gradient and Newton algorithms for updating the coordination vector. Derives the gradient matrix used in the Newton algorithm. Summarizes the steps of the new interaction prediction approach based on the proposed coordination scheme. Simulation Results Uses a continuous stirred tank reactor (CSTR) with a nonlinear fourth-order model as a benchmark problem. Presents the mathematical model of the reactor based on material and heat balances. Defines the state and input vectors and discretizes the system equations. Specifies the cost function and its parameters. Decomposes the overall problem into two sub-problems. Compares the convergence rate of the proposed approach to the classical mixed method. Shows the optimal states and inputs of the CSTR obtained using the proposed approach. Conclusion Summarizes the proposed two-level method for optimization of large-scale nonlinear systems. Highlights the faster convergence rate of the proposed approach compared to the classical mixed method. Emphasizes the reduced number of information exchanges between the two levels.}
