      \field{abstract}{Reasoning, the process of devising and executing complex goal-oriented action sequences, remains a critical challenge in AI. Current large language models (LLMs) primarily employ Chain-of-Thought (CoT) techniques, which suffer from brittle task decomposition, extensive data requirements, and high latency. Inspired by the hierarchical and multi-timescale processing in the human brain, we propose the Hierarchical Reasoning Model (HRM), a novel recurrent architecture that attains significant computational depth while maintaining both training stability and efficiency. HRM executes sequential reasoning tasks in a single forward pass without explicit supervision of the intermediate process, through two interdependent recurrent modules: a high-level module responsible for slow, abstract planning, and a low-level module handling rapid, detailed computations. With only 27 million parameters, HRM achieves exceptional performance on complex reasoning tasks using only 1000 training samples. The model operates without pre-training or CoT data, yet achieves nearly perfect performance on challenging tasks including complex Sudoku puzzles and optimal path finding in large mazes. Furthermore, HRM outperforms much larger models with significantly longer context windows on the Abstraction and Reasoning Corpus (ARC), a key benchmark for measuring artificial general intelligence capabilities. These results underscore HRM's potential as a transformative advancement toward universal computation and general-purpose reasoning systems.}
      \field{annotation}{Methods \& Technical Approach: HRM is composed of four modules: input network, low-level recurrent module, high-level recurrent module , and output network. Dynamics unfold over N high-level cycles, each with T low-level timesteps. Low-level state ​ converges toward an equilibrium conditioned​ during each cycle; high-level state updates once per cycle. Gradient computation uses a local approximation scheme that requires only O(1) memory and avoids time unrolling. Implemented efficiently in PyTorch with iterative local updates. Relevance to UHRC Project: Provides a unified computational model bridging high-level planning and low-level regulation—directly analogous to replacing multi-layer hierarchical control with a single end-to-end reasoning model. The temporal separation of fast vs. slow modules mirrors the supervisory vs. local controller roles in control theory. HRM’s efficient gradient approximation makes large-scale end-to-end control training feasible without BPTT’s memory bottlenecks. Suggests a direct path for implementing your Unified Hierarchical Reasoning Controller (UHRC), where supervisory planning and actuation-level regulation emerge in one integrated architecture. Strengths \& Limitations: Strengths: biologically inspired design, efficient gradient computation, improved depth of reasoning, unification of timescales. Limitations: not yet tested on physical control plants; focus primarily on reasoning and cognitive tasks; requires further validation in closed-loop control. Possible Follow-ups for Your Project: Adapt HRM’s dual-timescale dynamics (N high-level, T low-level) to control tasks with explicit separation of planning and regulation. Use HRM’s memory-efficient gradient scheme to train controllers on long-horizon simulations without GPU bottlenecks. Investigate “hierarchical convergence” as an alternative to layered PI/SMC architectures in nonlinear benchmark plants. Evaluate stability and robustness properties of HRM when applied to closed-loop control (not yet explored in the original work).}
